        <div id="how-we-chose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. How We Chose These Pads</h2>
<p>
            We have not tested these products. They were chosen from published specifications, the
            makers' own product pages and product guides, distributor and retailer listings, and
            independent coverage, and every claim on the cards is limited to what those sources
            support.
          </p>
          <p>
            We started from the three materials that matter: Sorbothane, the viscoelastic urethane
            used in purpose-made isolation pads; rubber and rubber-cork pads of the kind used under
            heavy equipment; and dense rubber mats, which do a similar job across the whole
            footprint. For each we looked for a product that is on sale now, whose maker or a major
            seller describes its size and material, and that a second source confirms. For the two
            Sorbothane pads we checked Isolate It's listings against Sorbothane, Inc.'s own product
            page and its Standard Products Guide. For the DiversiTech pad we compared several HVAC
            distributors. For the mats we checked the maker's or retailer's listing against a second
            retailer or an independent review.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>What we left out, and why.</strong> Load ratings were the most inconsistent
            figures in the category. One marketplace listing for a 4 x 4-inch Sorbothane pad claims a
            load rating in the thousands of pounds per pad; Sorbothane's own guide gives no rating
            for that size; and DiversiTech's pad is listed at 50 psi by some sellers and 180 psi by
            another. We have left every load rating off the cards. We excluded Isolate It's Industrial
            Silent Feet, because the maker's own recommended maximum for a set of four is 200 lb,
            which a treadmill and its user can exceed on their own, and its washing-machine pads,
            which are designed for a different machine. We also left out pads we could only find on
            marketplace listings with no maker information, a rubber pad set whose only source was
            its own seller, and a rubber-cork pad whose listings disagreed on pack count.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Safety check.</strong> We searched the US Consumer Product Safety Commission's
            recall database for every brand and seller on this page — Sorbothane, Isolate It,
            DiversiTech, FlooringInc and Tractor Supply. None of these products is under a recall or
            a CPSC warning; Tractor Supply's recalls in the database concern unrelated products.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-pads-can-do" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. What Pads Can and Cannot Do</h2>
<p>
            A treadmill makes two kinds of noise, and pads only touch one of them. <strong>Airborne
            noise</strong> — motor whine, belt slap, the sound of your feet in the room — travels
            through the air, and nothing under the feet changes it. <strong>Structure-borne
            noise</strong> is vibration passed from the frame into the floor, which the floor then
            radiates as sound, often in a room below or next door. That is the part a pad can reduce.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">How a pad works, in plain terms</h3>
          <p>
            An isolation pad is a soft spring and a shock absorber in one small block. It lets the
            machine move very slightly on top of it, and soaks up some of that movement as heat
            instead of passing it straight into the floor. It works best on fast, small vibrations —
            the steady buzz of a motor, the chatter of a belt, a frame rattling against a hard
            floor. Those can be noticeably reduced.
          </p>
          <p>
            It works least well on slow, heavy impacts, and footfall is exactly that. A runner lands
            two or three times a second, and each landing pushes far more force through the deck than
            the machine's weight alone. To isolate a slow thump like that, a pad would have to be so
            soft that the treadmill bounced visibly every stride — which would be unpleasant and
            unsafe. So every pad sold for this job is a compromise: firm enough to keep the machine
            steady, soft enough to take the edge off the higher-pitched part of each thud.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What to expect</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>On a concrete floor, pads mainly stop rattles and protect the floor. Little reaches neighbours anyway.</li>
            <li>On a timber floor, pads can soften the hum and the sharp edge of footfalls in the room below. The dull thud of running remains.</li>
            <li>Walking produces far less impact than running, and pads make a proportionally bigger difference to a walking treadmill or walking pad.</li>
            <li>A dense mat under the whole machine often helps more than pads alone, because it spreads the load as well as damping it.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            If the noise you are trying to fix is a knock, thump or squeak that the machine itself
            makes, a pad will not cure it. Our guide to
            <a href="/treadmill-thumping-noise/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill thumping noises</a>
            covers the mechanical causes worth ruling out first.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="stability" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Stability Comes First</h2>
<p>
            Anything placed under a treadmill changes how it stands, and the first job of a pad is
            not to make that worse. A treadmill that rocks, shifts or sits off level is a safety
            problem and a wear problem: the belt drifts to one side, the frame flexes, and a stumble
            on a moving belt is a genuinely dangerous thing.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Use identical pads under every contact point.</strong> The same material, the
            same thickness, the same hardness. Mixing a firm pad at the front with a soft one at the
            back, or a thick one with a thin one, tilts the deck and can twist the frame. If a
            treadmill rests on more than four points — many walking pads do — every one of them
            needs a pad, or none should.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Do not stack pads.</strong> Two pads on top of each other double the height and
            more than double the room for movement, and stacked soft pads are the most common way a
            stable machine becomes a wobbly one.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Prefer firm over thick.</strong> A firmer, thinner pad keeps the machine steady
            and still does useful work on vibration. A thick, soft pad looks like it should be
            better and usually is not, because the deck sinks and rocks under each stride. If you
            notice the treadmill moving on its pads when you run, the pad is too soft or too thick
            for the load.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Keep them where they belong.</strong> A pad that has crept half out from under a
            foot is now holding up one edge of the machine. Check the pads after the first few
            sessions and periodically after that, and re-level the treadmill once the pads have
            settled. If the machine shakes or wobbles even after you have levelled it on its pads,
            take them out and work through our guide to a
            <a href="/treadmill-shaking-or-wobbling/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill that shakes or wobbles</a>
            before going any further; pads should never be used to disguise a loose frame or a
            failing part.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="choosing-pads" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Choosing Size, Thickness and Hardness</h2>
<p>
            Three numbers describe an isolation pad: its footprint, its thickness and its hardness.
            Getting them roughly right matters more than the brand.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Footprint</h3>
          <p>
            The pad should be larger than the foot sitting on it, so the whole foot is supported
            and the pad does not roll at the edges. Look underneath your treadmill before you buy.
            Many folding treadmills stand on two adjustable rear feet and two front points at the
            motor end, often wheels or a frame rail. A 4 x 4-inch pad covers most adjustable feet
            with room to spare. Walking pads usually sit on a row of small rubber feet along the
            base, which suits smaller pads, one per foot.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Thickness</h3>
          <p>
            Thicker pads isolate more, but they also let the machine move more and raise it further.
            Sorbothane's own product guide shows the trade-off clearly: for the same footprint and
            hardness, the recommended load per pad falls as the pad gets thicker. In other words, a
            thick pad is meant for a lighter load. For a treadmill, which carries a person as well as
            its own weight, thin-to-moderate thicknesses are the sensible range; the 1/4 to 1/2-inch
            Sorbothane pads on this page sit in it.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Hardness</h3>
          <p>
            Sorbothane is sold in durometers — a measure of softness — and the three common grades
            are 30, 50 and 70. Lower numbers are softer and carry less load; 70 is the firmest. For a
            treadmill with a runner on it, the firmer grades are generally the safer starting point,
            because a soft grade under a heavy, moving load is where rocking begins. Rubber and
            rubber-cork pads are firmer still, which makes them stable but less effective on fine
            vibration.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Load ratings</h3>
          <p>
            Sorbothane's approach to sizing is simple: divide the total weight by the number of
            pads to get the load on each, then choose a pad rated for that load. The difficulty for
            treadmills is that the load is not constant — each footfall briefly adds far more than
            body weight — and that published ratings for the larger pads are missing or
            inconsistent, as we found. Treat any single load figure on a listing with caution, start
            with a firmer grade, and judge by whether the machine stays steady.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="pads-vs-mat" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Pads, a Mat, or Both</h2>
<p>
            Pads and mats overlap, and the right answer depends on the floor and on what you are
            trying to fix.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Pads</strong> concentrate their effect where the machine touches the floor. They
            are small, cheap, easy to hide and good at stopping rattles and reducing motor hum. They
            do nothing to protect the floor from sweat, belt dust or dropped water bottles, and on
            carpet they can sink unevenly into the pile.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A dense rubber mat</strong> spreads the load across a much bigger area, protects
            the floor, keeps carpet fibre away from the motor, and does a similar damping job. It is
            heavier, more expensive and more visible. For a treadmill upstairs, a thick dense mat is
            usually the bigger single improvement; our
            <a href="/best-treadmill-mats/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmill mats</a>
            guide covers the thinner floor-protection mats too, and our guide to
            <a href="/how-thick-should-a-treadmill-mat-be/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how thick a treadmill mat should be</a>
            explains the trade-offs of going thicker.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Both together</strong> can work, with care. A firm pad under each foot, on top of
            a dense rubber mat, adds a second layer of isolation. But every soft layer adds
            movement, so keep the pads firm and thin if you combine them, and check the machine is
            still level and steady. Do not put pads on top of a soft foam mat; that is two soft
            layers and a recipe for rocking.
          </p>
          <p>
            On carpet, a mat is the better choice. Pads pressed into carpet pile can sink at
            different rates and tilt the machine.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="fitting" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Fitting Pads Without Making Things Worse</h2>
<p>
            Fitting pads takes ten minutes, and most problems afterwards come from rushing it.
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Unplug the treadmill</strong> and remove the safety key. Treat it as you would any other maintenance job.</li>
            <li><strong>Look underneath</strong> and identify every point that touches the floor, including front wheels or frame rails that bear weight when the machine is in use.</li>
            <li><strong>Lift one end at a time</strong>, with a helper for anything heavier than a walking pad, and slide a pad centrally under each contact point at that end. Never put a hand under a raised frame without someone holding it.</li>
            <li><strong>Lower it gently</strong> and check each pad is still centred. Repeat at the other end.</li>
            <li><strong>Level the machine on its pads</strong> using the rear adjustment feet, then walk on it at low speed and watch for rocking or the belt drifting off centre.</li>
            <li><strong>Check again after a week</strong>. Sorbothane and rubber settle slightly under load; re-centre any pad that has crept and re-level if needed.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            Folding treadmills need one extra thought. When you fold the deck and wheel the machine
            away, loose pads stay behind and may not be in quite the right place when you set it up
            again. Either leave the pads where the feet land and check them each time, or choose a
            fabric-faced pad, which is designed to be repositioned without sticking. Some raw
            Sorbothane pads are slightly tacky, which helps them stay put but can mark some floor
            finishes; if your floor is pale wood or vinyl, check under the pads after the first few
            weeks.
          </p>
          <p>
            For a walking pad, which is light and often moved daily, a thin mat cut to the
            footprint is usually more practical than a set of loose pads. Our
            <a href="/best-quiet-walking-pads/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">quietest walking pads</a>
            guide covers the machine side of that choice.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="downstairs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. If the Complaint Comes From Downstairs</h2>
<p>
            The most common reason people search for anti-vibration pads is a neighbour, and it is
            worth being realistic before you spend anything.
          </p>
          <p>
            Ask someone to stand in the room below while you walk and then run. If what they hear is
            a hum or a whine, pads under the feet will help noticeably. If it is a rhythmic thud
            that matches your stride, pads will soften it but not remove it, and a thick dense mat
            under the whole machine is the better first step. If the thud is loud even when you
            walk, the floor itself is the issue: lightweight timber floors carry impact well, and no
            product sold for this job changes that much.
          </p>
          <p>
            What helps most, roughly in order: walking rather than running, a smoother running
            style with shorter, lighter strides, a dense rubber mat, firm isolation pads, and the
            time of day you train. The machine matters too. Heavier, more rigid treadmills generally
            transmit less rattle than light ones, and some motors are simply quieter. Our guides to
            <a href="/quiet-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">quiet treadmills</a>
            and the
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-apartments/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmills for apartments</a>
            cover the choices that make the biggest difference before you ever reach for a pad.
          </p>
          <p>
            Check your lease or building rules as well. Some buildings set quiet hours or flooring
            requirements, and a short conversation with the neighbour below about when you train
            will often do more than any pad.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Who Should Buy Something Else</h2>
<p>
            <strong>If your treadmill is on a ground-floor concrete slab</strong>, vibration has
            nowhere much to go. A floor-protection mat is useful; isolation pads add little.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If the noise is coming from the machine</strong> — a squeak, a knock, a motor
            that sounds strained — fix the cause first. Pads will hide nothing that matters, and some
            of those noises are early signs of wear.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If the treadmill is on thick carpet</strong>, choose a heavy rubber mat, and on
            deep pile a rigid board beneath it, rather than pads that sink into the pile.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you want a quiet machine for an upstairs flat and have not bought one yet</strong>,
            spend the time on the treadmill rather than the accessories. A walking pad used for
            walking, or a heavier treadmill with a well-supported deck, will usually do more for the
            people below than anything you can put under a lighter machine.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do anti-vibration pads work for treadmills?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, within limits. Pads reduce the vibration a treadmill passes into the floor, which softens motor hum, rattles and the sharper part of each footfall. They do not stop the heavy thud of running reaching the room below, and they do nothing for noise in the room itself. They make the biggest difference to walking treadmills and walking pads on timber floors.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are Sorbothane pads better than rubber pads under a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Sorbothane is designed to absorb vibration rather than simply cushion, so for the same thickness it generally does more for hum and rattle than plain rubber. Rubber and rubber-cork pads are firmer and cheaper, which makes them very stable. For a running treadmill, a firm grade of Sorbothane is a good balance; rubber-cork is the budget alternative.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will anti-vibration pads stop my downstairs neighbors hearing my treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They will reduce what your neighbours hear, not stop it. The rhythmic thud of running is a slow, heavy impact that no pad can fully absorb without letting the treadmill bounce. A thick dense rubber mat usually helps more than pads alone. Walking rather than running, training at sensible hours and choosing a quieter machine make the biggest difference.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How thick should treadmill vibration pads be?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a treadmill with a runner on it, thin to moderate pads, roughly 1/4 to 1/2 inch for Sorbothane, are the sensible range. Thicker, softer pads isolate more in theory but let the machine move and rock. Every contact point needs an identical pad, and you should re-level the treadmill after fitting them and check the belt still runs centrally.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I put pads under a treadmill mat or on top of it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                If you use both, the mat goes on the floor and firm, thin pads go on top of it under each foot. That adds a second layer of isolation. Keep the pads firm, because every soft layer adds movement, and never put pads on a soft foam mat. On carpet, use a dense rubber mat alone rather than pads.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can anti-vibration pads make a treadmill unsafe?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They can if they are too soft, too thick, mismatched or out of position. A treadmill that rocks or shifts on its pads can let the belt drift and makes a stumble more likely. Use identical firm pads under every contact point, do not stack them, level the machine after fitting, and remove them if the treadmill moves while you run.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes With Treadmill Anti-Vibration Pads</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Each of these turns a cheap fix into one that either does nothing or makes the treadmill less safe.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the softest, thickest pad</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Soft, thick pads look like they should absorb the most, but under a moving runner they let the deck sink and rock with every stride. That is uncomfortable, wears the frame and belt, and makes a stumble more likely. Choose a firm grade and a moderate thickness, and judge success by whether the machine stays steady, not by how squashy the pad feels.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Padding only some of the feet</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Putting pads under the two rear feet and leaving the front on the floor tilts the deck and twists the frame. The same goes for mixing pad types or thicknesses. Every point that carries weight when the treadmill is running needs an identical pad. Then level the machine on its pads and check that the belt still tracks centrally.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting pads to silence running upstairs</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Pads soften hum and rattle and take the edge off footfalls, but the slow thud of a runner landing is more than any pad can absorb without the treadmill bouncing. If the complaint is a rhythmic thump, add a thick dense rubber mat, walk rather than run at sensitive times, and talk to the neighbour about when you train.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Never checking the pads again</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Pads settle and creep, especially under folding treadmills that are moved and set up again. A pad that has slid half out from under a foot is holding one corner of the machine on its edge. Check the pads after the first few sessions and every few weeks after that, re-centre any that have moved, and re-level the treadmill if needed.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

